Each section is uniform in width, so each part of the graph is straight; only the steepness changes abruptly where the container's width changes.

mastery.question_text:
Sketch a graph for the population of rabbits introduced to an island: it starts at a small, roughly constant number, then grows increasingly quickly once the rabbits are established, then its growth slows as the island's food supply becomes limited, before finally levelling off completely. Label the axes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.3);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.2,0);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.15) {Rabbit population};
\node[font=\small] at (2.6,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.3);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.2,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0.3) -- (0.8,0.3);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+0.8},{0.3+0.5*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+2.0},{1.02+(-0.35*(\x-1.4)*(\x-1.4)+0.686)});
\draw[thick,blue] (3.4,1.706) -- (4.2,1.706);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.15) {Rabbit population};
\node[font=\small] at (2.6,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Stage 1: the population stays roughly constant while it is small — a flat line.
Stage 2: once established, the population grows faster and faster — a curve that rises and gets steeper, increasing at an increasing rate.
Stage 3: as food becomes limited, growth slows — the curve keeps rising but gets flatter, increasing at a decreasing rate.
Stage 4: the population levels off completely — a flat line again.

